IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Row il-50. NOUNS of a printed date pair and of a run of section numbers. No rule is written here; the facts below are what a reader of the texts could state.
-- TARGET COMPILER: main. No REFUSE, no section GIVEN, no TYPICALLY, no YMD, no sets: a case the sources do not answer is NOTHING from a MAYBE.
--
-- This row is NOT an encoding of the Companies Law, 5759-1999, nor of the two sets of regulations it quotes. It records three places where the words of an instrument disagree with themselves
-- or with a later instrument: two date pairs whose Hebrew half and Gregorian half name different days, and one saving clause that keeps Ordinance sections which a later law cancelled.
-- A record whose two halves differ is a defect, and is kept in an expected-red file.
--
-- THE KINDS BELOW ARE MINE, not the texts': each is a short plain description I wrote from reading the words. The words of the texts are quoted beside each value in the references module
-- (`ext:[fees]`, `ext:[compromise]`, `ext:[comp]` and `ext:[ord]` lines are the deposited Hebrew Wikisource excerpts; `ext:[SH ...]` and `ext:[KT ...]` lines are typed from a page image
-- of the official instrument).

§ `Nouns of where a reading comes from`

-- Four kinds of reading. The first three run from the strongest to the weakest; the fourth is the text layer of an official PDF, whose words and numerals can be checked and whose order of words cannot.
-- The second kind is the honest name for most limbs here: the official page was read, but that no LATER instrument touched the words rests on the consolidation's list of amending instruments.
DECLARE `Where a reading comes from` IS ONE OF
    `the official instrument, read from its page image`
    `the official instrument read from its page image; that no later instrument touched the words is CONSOLIDATION-ONLY`
    `the consolidation only`
    `the text layer of the official instrument, not the page image`

§ `Nouns of a printed date pair`

-- A date pair is a Hebrew date and a Gregorian date printed side by side as one date. The Gregorian day the Hebrew date computes to is NOT computed by L4: it is a fact typed from a calendar routine
-- that reproduced 30 Kislev 5785 = 31 December 2024 before it was used (NOTES.md, fork D1).
DECLARE `A printed date pair` HAS
    `the pair is printed in` IS A STRING
    `what the date fixes` IS A STRING
    `the Hebrew date as printed` IS A STRING
    `the Gregorian day as printed` IS A DATE
    `the Gregorian day the Hebrew date computes to` IS A DATE
    `the pair was read from` IS A `Where a reading comes from`

§ `Nouns of a run of section numbers`

-- A run is the printed ends of a range of section numbers ("164 to 201"). Whether every number in a run is a section of the Ordinance is not recorded: the row counts the NUMBERS the printed ranges name.
DECLARE `A run of section numbers` HAS
    `the run is printed in` IS A STRING
    `first number` IS A NUMBER
    `last number` IS A NUMBER
    `the ends of the run were read from` IS A `Where a reading comes from`
